---
title: "Accessibility diagnostics demo"
---

This page deliberately contains accessibility problems so both the preview's
client-side a11y audit (the dev-menu panel) and the static `qmd-fast check` a11y gate
(`validate_a11y`) have something to flag. It lives in `corpus/diagnostics/`, which is
exempt from the corpus clean-render guards. Each located finding (the ones tied to a
block) jumps to its source line on click, like any other diagnostic.

## A section with a raw image

<img src="logo.png">

The raw `<img>` above has no `alt` attribute, so a screen reader can't describe it
(a decorative image should use `alt=""`).

#### A subsection that skips a heading level

This heading is an `h4` directly under the `h2` above, skipping `h3`.

Here is [](#) — a link with no text, so it has no accessible name. And here is a
<button></button> with no label, which a screen reader announces as just "button".

Finally, a <div role="button"></div> and a <span role="link"></span>: ARIA roles make a
plain element interactive, but with no accessible name a screen reader announces them as a
bare "button" / "link" (the static gate flags `[role=button|link|tab]` without a name too).
